{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.68,"content_digest":"c5ba6fdf135f7591055220f4fed6ef641991542cc0f3f3a2ed05daa18eda5f99","created_at":"2026-08-22T18:13:03+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"5de1c3aca75bc41e17b48c7b3e1e30e4925a966849def0ebd9633a675bb93304","input_tokens":5648,"latency_ms":30113,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"c4a459e6f410f8e38d658768ee9be37053115e86e617c12f69aa04b6c3bef21d","output_tokens":2470,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:23f8b4254a07bd1b788d22c8","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:data-quality-ceiling","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:scientific-discovery:01M0NASA8ER1QB6Q3M781TE3QX","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The data-quality-ceiling principle bounds the SSL claim: any performance gain is limited by the information and biases in the available labeled and unlabeled data. SSL can supplement scarce labels but cannot recover distinctions entirely absent from the data, so the reported gains are conditional on the data distribution and quality.","relation_type":"bounded_by","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T02:58:57+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0P5WC937PV7PKQMASBZ4D3Z"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T18:13:03+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.75,"content_digest":"3a18f6f55bd67538ae9678f36fb5e276aed9abca9e3ed45fc744e7522b51b3cc","created_at":"2026-08-22T03:44:27+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[],"link_id":"foundation-link:ca744c7e14f25088375320ae","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:credit-assignment","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:general:01M0B1JQ8GAFJRYRFF288V2N0J","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The meta-principle that learning requires assigning outcomes to responsible internal decisions supports the claim that dense verifier-derived rewards improve sample efficiency and final success. Dense rewards provide more frequent and informative credit signals than sparse rewards, which is a direct application of improved credit assignment in reinforcement learning.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T04:12:58+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0KTQZ6M68SKHPKARZR7EKFP"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T03:44:27+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"partial","confidence":0.7,"content_digest":"1a3f571e44942f907429031c3df7c070df5a9c260228d44f7dd7b85cf71c5679","created_at":"2026-08-23T07:02:07+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"f4ce88985f3d74410d5db3ceb47104c1d152aa74e7821e822bf78a56fe487730","input_tokens":5890,"latency_ms":33192,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"84613198a4c3fc70c5bc4de5e4d7a4aeb384b452555e522f21bb892165469dd0","output_tokens":2627,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:d83f1eb6d79b4ce4dc244162","meta_principle_id":"meta:economics-game-theory:nash-equilibrium","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:scientific-discovery:01M0PPSDF7VG785S8PVM11JJJR","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"Both the Nash equilibrium theorem and the minimax-regret equilibrium theorem are equilibrium-existence results for finite games when mixed strategies are allowed. They share the same strategic self-consistency structure and finite-game/mixed-strategy scale, although the solution concept and informational assumptions differ. The analogy is structural, not a claim that minimax-regret equilibria are Nash equilibria.","relation_type":"analogous_to","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T08:08:08+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0PTK9G6V6VXNVFWAWBHMSYT"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T07:02:07+00:00","variable_compatibility":"partial"}
{"condition_compatibility":"partial","confidence":0.55,"content_digest":"797719b4df659ea5159a56f317d8e03d5e5b2f2f1feac337deea68063c82f539","created_at":"2026-08-23T06:28:55+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"59ef0fa8181a6d7bc0dfebb17d762eb1c31459cbd5361b949f41a324ff31ccf9","input_tokens":5585,"latency_ms":32235,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"0e985dcac00cc7537171443ae366820938b368aecf5b825990e968458c7eceb4","output_tokens":2396,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:e1ef7f9f12682d52c9943abb","meta_principle_id":"meta:statistics-causality:markov-equivalence","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:socio-technical-systems:01M0PMWNCXF95DN340BXTR6NNJ","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"Markov equivalence shows that purely observational data generally identify an equivalence class of causal structures rather than a unique internal DAG. Analogously, provenance logs of ML inputs and outputs are observational traces that can document external data flow but do not uniquely determine the algorithm's internal reasoning states.","relation_type":"analogous_to","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T08:08:08+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0PTK9G6V6VXNVFWAWBHMSYT"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T06:28:55+00:00","variable_compatibility":"partial"}
